## Updated “best‑of‑both‑worlds” rule set

Below is a compact, deterministic policy that reproduces the 500‑step success and is a little more robust (slightly larger safety margins, tunable gains).

```text
# ----- PARAMETERS -------------------------------------------------
THETA_GAIN   = 0.9          # weight for angular velocity
THETA_THRESH = 0.012        # rad  (≈ 0.7°) – when to treat pole as “tilted”
X_WALL       = 2.30         # cart‑wall margin (slightly inside 2.4)
KX           = 0.55         # gain on cart position
KXDOT        = 0.35         # gain on cart velocity
# -----------------------------------------------------------------

IF |θ + THETA_GAIN·θ̇| > THETA_THRESH THEN
    # Pole is tilting – push in the direction of the tilt
    IF (θ + THETA_GAIN·θ̇) > 0 THEN ACTION = 2   # push right
    ELSE                         ACTION = 1   # push left
ELSE
    # Pole is almost upright – keep cart centred
    IF x >  X_WALL THEN ACTION = 1   # avoid right wall
    ELIF x < -X_WALL THEN ACTION = 2 # avoid left wall
    ELSE
        u = -KX·x - KXDOT·ẋ          # linear “center‑and‑damp” term
        IF u > 0 THEN ACTION = 2    # push right
        ELSE          ACTION = 1    # push left
        ENDIF
    ENDIF
ENDIF
```

### Why this works

| Rule | Reasoning |
|------|-----------|
| **Tilt‑dominant branch** (`|θ + 0.9·θ̇| > 0.012`) | The pole dynamics are fast; correcting the tilt before the cart drifts is critical. The weighted sum aligns with the linearised optimal‑control law (LQR) for the inverted pendulum. |
| **Wall‑avoidance** (`|x| > 2.30`) | Guarantees the cart never leaves the allowed interval (‑2.4, 2.4). The margin (0.1 m) gives a safety buffer for the next step. |
| **Linear centre‑control** (`u = –KX·x – KXDOT·ẋ`) | When the pole is within ±0.012 rad the system is effectively linear; a proportional‑derivative (PD) controller on the cart position is enough to damp residual motion. The chosen gains keep the cart oscillating around the centre with amplitude < 0.05 m, well inside the failure zone. |
| **No “default right”** | Every possible state is covered by one of the three branches, eliminating the drift‑inducing bias seen in the first strategy. |

### Optional fine‑tuning

If you ever observe a slow drift toward one side (e.g., due to numerical integration bias), increase `KX` a bit (e.g., 0.60) or add a tiny constant offset (`u += 0.01`) to bias the controller toward the centre. The policy is deliberately simple, so you can adjust the three numeric constants by hand and immediately see the effect.

