### 2. Updated (and simplified) rule set  

Below is a clean, deterministic policy that reproduces the perfect‑score behaviour and explicitly handles every possible state value.

```text
// ------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Compute the “tilt‑error” term
s = θ + 0.9 * θ̇               // θ  = pole angle (rad)
// ------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Strong correction when the pole is noticeably tilting
IF |s| > 0.013 THEN
    IF s > 0      THEN action = 2   // push right
    ELSE               action = 1   // push left
ELSE
    // ------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Quiet region – keep the cart centred
    IF x >  0.05   THEN action = 1   // cart too far right → push left
    ELSE IF x < -0.05 THEN action = 2   // cart too far left  → push right
    ELSE
        // 4️⃣  Exactly (or almost) centred – choose a deterministic bias
        //    (any constant direction works; we keep the proven “right” bias)
        action = 2                 // push right
```

**Why this works**

| Step | What it does | Why it helps |
|------|--------------|--------------|
| 1️⃣   | `s = θ + 0.9·θ̇` condenses angle + angular‑velocity into a single “danger” metric. | The pole’s dynamics are linearised around the upright position; this combination predicts the future angle a few timesteps ahead. |
| 2️⃣   | If `|s|` exceeds 0.013 we apply a *strong* push in the direction the pole is falling. | The pole is already gaining momentum; a weak correction would be too late. |
| 3️⃣   | When the pole is almost upright we only care about the cart’s horizontal offset. | Small cart drifts do not endanger the pole, but they must be corrected before they grow large enough to hit the track limits. |
| 4️⃣   | If the cart is exactly centred we always push right. | Guarantees a concrete action (no “None”) and reproduces the bias that the perfect‑score runs used. The bias does not destabilise the system because the pole is already balanced. |

