### 1.1  Why the “best” strategy works  

| Observation | What we saw in the trace | Why the rule behaved correctly |
|-------------|--------------------------|--------------------------------|
| **θ + 0.9 ω** is a good proxy for the *future* angle. | Whenever `|θ+0.9 ω|` crossed the 0.013‑threshold the pole was already tilting noticeably. The rule immediately pushed the cart in the direction that reduces the tilt (sign‑dependent). | The coefficient 0.9 is close to the optimal gain obtained by linear‑quadratic regulation for the classic cart‑pole dynamics. It gives a predictive “error” that already incorporates the angular velocity, so the controller reacts before the pole falls. |
| **Tight position band (±0.05 m)** only activates when the pole is almost upright. | In the last 20 steps the cart never left the ±0.05 m window; the controller stayed in the “angle‑only” mode. | By keeping the cart near the centre we avoid hitting the ±2.4 m failure bound while we are already stabilising the pole. The band is wide enough to let the pole‑controller do its job, but narrow enough to pull the cart back when it drifts. |
