### 4️⃣ Updated rule set (the final policy)

```text
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
//  CART‑POLE BALANCING POLICY
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Variables (from the observation vector)
//   x   = cart position            (m)   –2.4 … 2.4
//   v   = cart velocity            (m/s)
//   θ   = pole angle               (rad) –0.2095 … 0.2095
//   ω   = pole angular velocity    (rad/s)

// Helper term – “lean‑momentum”
lean = θ + 0.9 * ω          // ≈ linearised pole dynamics

// 1️⃣ Strong lean → act on the pole
if (abs(lean) > 0.013) {
    if (lean > 0) {
        action = 2;        // push right → counteract right‑lean
    } else {
        action = 1;        // push left  → counteract left‑lean
    }
}
// 2️⃣ Near‑vertical (dead‑zone) → keep cart roughly centred
else {
    // Small bias to bring the cart back to the centre if it drifts
    if (x > 0.05) {
        action = 1;        // cart too far right → push left
    } else if (x < -0.05) {
        action = 2;        // cart too far left  → push right
    } else {
        // deterministic tie‑break – any direction works, choose right
        action = 2;
    }
}
```

#### Why this works
* **`lean`** captures the combined effect of angle and angular velocity, which is exactly the quantity the linearised inverted‑pendulum dynamics try to drive to zero.
* The threshold **0.013 rad** (≈ 0.75°) is just large enough to ignore measurement noise but small enough to react before the pole diverges.
* The optional *x*‑bias prevents the cart from slowly wandering to the track edges; it only activates when the cart is > 5 cm off‑centre, far inside the safe interval (±2.4 m).
* No explicit velocity check is needed – the pole controller already damps cart motion.

### 5️⃣ What to try next (optional refinements)

| Idea | Reasoning | Expected impact |
|------|-----------|-----------------|
| **Adaptive threshold** (`τ = 0.013 * (1 + |x|)`) | When the cart is near the edge we may want to be a bit more aggressive. | Slightly higher safety margin, negligible extra complexity. |
| **Randomised tie‑break** (`action = rand(1,2)`) | Removes any tiny bias that could accumulate over many episodes. | Same performance; useful if we ever see systematic drift. |
| **Add a tiny integral term** (`lean += 0.02 * θ`) | Compensates for very slow bias in the pole angle that the proportional term might miss. | Could improve robustness on noisy simulators. |

