### 1️⃣ What the data tell us  

| Strategy | Typical average reward (20‑run) | Max reward observed |
|----------|--------------------------------|---------------------|
| **Rule 1‑4 (4‑rule cascade)** | 472 / 500 | 500 (rare) |
| **Previous 3‑rule version** | 465 / 500 | 500 (rare) |
| **Best‑so‑far rule set** | **500 / 500** (every trial) | 500 (every trial) |

The **best‑so‑far rule set** is the one that *always* reached the episode horizon (500 steps).  
All three rule families share the same **pole‑error term**  

\[
e = \theta + k\;\omega\qquad (k\approx0.9)
\]

and they all use a **dead‑band** on this error (|e| > 0.013) to decide whether to act on the pole or on the cart.  
The differences lie only in the “cart‑centering” part:

| Variant | Cart‑centering logic |
|---------|----------------------|
| 4‑rule cascade | centre → velocity damping → deterministic right push |
| 3‑rule version | centre → deterministic right push |
| Best‑so‑far | centre → **large dead‑band (±0.05 m)** → deterministic right push |

**Why the “large” dead‑band works better**

* When the pole is already well‑balanced (|e| ≤ 0.013) the cart can wander a little without threatening the pole.  
* By allowing the cart to drift up to ±0.05 m before we start “steering” it back, we avoid **unnecessary corrective pushes** that would re‑excite the pole.  
* The deterministic tie‑break (push right) is harmless because it is only used when the cart is *exactly* in the centre and has zero velocity – a state that is already stable.

The extra velocity‑damping rule in the 4‑rule cascade actually hurts performance: it forces a push opposite to the direction of motion even when the pole is stable, which injects extra energy into the system and sometimes leads to a premature failure.

---

