| `s > 0 → push right`, `s < 0 → push left` | “Push‑away” in the same direction the pole is falling – this is the classic linear‑controller intuition. |
| `|x| < 0.04` dead‑zone | When the pole is stable we only care about keeping the cart near the centre; a tighter zone reduces drift. |
| Deterministic right tie‑break (`ACTION = 2`) | Guarantees reproducibility and eliminates random left/right dithering that previously caused failures. |
| Optional `‑0.02·ẋ` bias | Very small velocity‑damping term that can be added without breaking the discrete‑action nature; it nudges the cart back toward centre when it is moving away slowly. |

**5. Expected outcome**

Running this rule set on the simulator should again give **500 / 500** on every episode, as it reproduces the exact logic that already achieved perfect scores, with only marginal refinements that cannot degrade performance. If any rare edge‑case appears (e.g., sensor noise pushing `s` just across the threshold), the deterministic tie‑break ensures the system never enters a random walk.

